  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Tuesday: Forecast remains on track for today for \nelevated to locally critical fire weather conditions for the \nOkanogan valley despite being cooler today. Boundary layer winds are \nmuch weaker than yesterday but with slope alignment again, there is \n20-40% chance of briefly meeting red flag warning criteria this \nafternoon and early evening for the Okanogan valley. Dewpoints west \nof US-395 are 5-15 degrees drier than yesterday as drier air has \nfiltered into central WA being brushed by a decaying cold front, so \nrelative humidities will dip into the 10-20% range this afternoon. \nFurther east in eastern WA and north ID, conditions are cool thanks \nto cloud cover from monsoonal moisture. The airmass moisture content \nin place hardly changes into tomorrow and Tuesday, but temperatures \nwill sharply rise into the 80s to low 100s as height rises take \nplace. HeatRisk will increase from minor today to moderate \nand locally major by Tuesday, affecting folks without proper \ncooling/hydration. Similar elevated to locally critical fire \nweather conditions are expected for the Okanogan valley Monday \nand Tuesday. \n\nWednesday through Sunday: Ensembles are into good agreement that a \ntrough will be off the coast Wednesday, but the evolution of it \nstill remains unclear. Ensembles are split essentially 50/50 with \nthis trough coming onshore or staying offshore by Thursday. \nUnsurprisingly, having the low offshore would keep stronger ridging \nover the area leading to temperatures in the low 90s to low 100s \nwith a 10-30% chance of drier thunderstorms. If the low were to come \nashore, temperatures would be much cooler in the 70s and 80s with \ngreater chances (20-40%) of slower, wetter thunderstorms. With any \nthreat of thunderstorms will come the threat of fire starts and \ngusty outflow winds. Forecast confidence actually is higher beyond \nmidweek for above average temperatures in the 90s to low 100s to \nreturn (or persist depending on how mid-week plays out) as high \npressure in the northern plains retrogrades west back into the \nintermountain west. /DB",
